// Regression case for the string-literal `\xHH` escape (commit fc46e9db).
//
// Cedar decodes `"\x41"` to the single character `A`. Dogwood must agree,
// since it shares Cedar's string-literal syntax. Before the fix,
// `decode_string` did not recognize `\x` at all and produced the four
// characters `\`, `x`, `4`, `1` instead — so this comparison silently never
// matched a real `document == "A"` event, flipping the verdict from `true`
// (permit) to `false` (deny).
//
// The trace supplies a Read whose document is exactly "A". With the fix the
// literal below IS "A", the guard holds, and the verdict is `true`. On the
// buggy prefix the literal is the 4-char string `\x41`, the guard fails, and
// the verdict is the wrong `false`.
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when { context.input.document == "\x41" };
